eloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069085/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cc6c4ac9399b9296d2b1e76b791951bc46ef846c7c14f3641854b754317ce8"} {"question" "q-278364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069085/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cc6c4ac9399b9296d2b1e76b791951bc46ef846c7c14f3641854b754317ce8"} {"question" "q-278364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-5 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =5\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,8)$ and $(0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069085/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cc6c4ac9399b9296d2b1e76b791951bc46ef846c7c14f3641854b754317ce8"} {"question" "q-278364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce2e4bb2ba352af617b4f6555fa31a21236b223839a0cb8256fa40d1cf64d5e8"} {"question" "q-278364" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-worke